\section{Why this function exists}{

Until this was centralised, \code{over_ball} had two incompatible definitions in
the codebase. The parser wrote \code{over + ball / 10} (and all 10,895,339 stored
rows use it, so every model was trained on that scale), while the prediction
and simulation paths reconstructed it as \code{over + ball / 6}. Over 10 ball 3
was therefore \code{10.3} at training time and \code{10.5} at scoring time, and the
derived \code{overs_left = max_overs - over_ball} inherited the error. Nothing
raised an error; predictions were simply scored on a feature scale the model
had never seen. One definition, called from every site, is what prevents
that recurring.
